2026-10-08 (wave 4, batch 1):

## Trimmed promise surface, cheaper value coercion, SIMD tiers proved on x86

Regression tests: tests/test_audit_w4_native_1.js, tests/test_simd_tiers.c,
tests/test_kit.js.

- REMOVED (each had a blocking form that stays): dyna:file readFileAsync,
  writeFileAsync, copyFileAsync, asyncStats and FileWriter.syncAsync;
  HTTPClient getAsync, postAsync, requestAsync; Fetcher.getAsync;
  Argon2id hashAsync, verifyAsync, asyncStats; os.sleepAsync (the global
  `sleep(ms)` stays). The servers, PostgreSQL, Redis, DNS, streams, spawn,
  the file watcher, fetch() and dyna:async are unchanged. For a request to a
  server in the same process use fetch(); hash passwords on an os.Worker when
  the event loop must keep serving. A Crawl still accepts a client object with
  its own `requestAsync(method, url)`.
- A value that is tested for "is this bytes?" no longer builds and discards
  an exception to find out. TextDecoder.decode(Uint8Array) 1903 -> 201 ns per
  call; ArrayBuffer arguments to the hashes, the codecs and dyna:bytes 1.8 us
  -> 0.1 us; CBOR and MessagePack encoding of plain objects 7.7x faster
  (20000 small objects 37 ms -> 2.8 ms) and structuredClone 8x;
  conn.write("text") on a TCP connection and an App handler that returns an
  object no longer raise two internal exceptions per call.
- TextDecoder skips its byte-wise state machine when the whole input
  validates as UTF-8: 4 MiB of ASCII 436 -> 6090 MB/s, a 200-byte message
  540 -> 78 ns. Malformed input takes the old path and gives the same text.
- dyna:bytes count(buf, byte) is one vectorised pass (502 MB/s -> memory
  speed on text with a space every fifth byte).
- dyna:simd: a value gives the same result at every position of an array on
  every tier. `silu` used the fast exponential in the vector body and expf in
  the last 1-3 elements (1.7% apart) and on the scalar tier; it is the fast
  form everywhere, as documented. On x86 `sigmoid`, `tanhFast` and `vexp`
  rounded differently in the vector body than in the tail and `sigmoid`
  did not saturate to exactly 0 below -30. `relu6(-0)` is +0 like `relu(-0)`.
- scrape: `new Extractor(spec)` released none of its field names (81 bytes
  per field for the life of the process).
- build.sh: `t SUITE... [--case=PAT]` runs named suites, and named cases of a
  tests/kit.js suite, against the binary that is already built and refuses a
  stale one. Every subcommand that builds or runs the binary holds one tree
  lock; a second one fails at once naming the owner instead of running
  underneath it. `test-simd-tiers` runs the element-wise kernels on this
  CPU's tier and `test-simd-x86` runs every kernel harness on the SSE4.2 and
  AVX2 tiers under Rosetta 2 on an Apple-silicon Mac.
- tests/kit.js: named cases, a case filter, assertions that report what they
  saw, loud skips, per-case timeouts.

2026-10-07 (batch 10):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 10: SIMD pre-scan, HTTP client, io_uring backend, installer)

Regression tests: tests/test_audit_w3_native_10.js, tests/test_install_flow.sh.

- HTTP client: response header lines with the same name are combined in
  order ("a=1, b=2") instead of keeping only the last, so no Set-Cookie line
  is lost. Over https a body with no declared length is delivered when the
  peer ends with close_notify and is a "truncated body" error when the stream
  is cut; it used to be the other way round (the clean close failed with
  "receive failed", the cut returned 200 with a partial body).
- dyna:simd: the NaN pre-scan of relu, clamp, sigmoid, vsqrt, max and min is
  vectorisable (relu 0.49 -> 0.16 ns per element, max/min 0.54 -> 0.20);
  `variance` is one pass with no temporary (0.375 -> 0.272, same bits).
- x86 dispatch: the AVX2 kernels are selected only when FMA is present and
  the AVX-512 kernels only when F, DQ and BW all are, which is what those
  files are compiled for.
- io_uring backend (Linux, CONFIG_IO_URING): a callback that grows the
  descriptor table no longer leaves the completion handler with a stale slot;
  a send buffer is kept until its completion arrives; sendfile closes the
  source descriptor on every refused call; disarming the periodic tick is
  silent. `dyna:uring` reads into zeroed memory and reaps outstanding block
  reads before freeing after an error. CONFIG_IO_URING with CONFIG_TLS is
  refused at configure time (it could not link).
- Files are read into the heap unless a caller asks for a mapping; no module
  asks.
- install.sh: `--uninstall` removes only files that identify as DynaJS; an
  unset HOME is refused with a message.
- build.sh: CONFIG_NATIVE uses -march=native on x86; the hardening-flag
  cache is keyed on compiler identity, target and the probe list. The
  pre-push hook also gates changes to build.sh, tools/, install.sh and
  dynajs.d.ts.

BEHAVIOR CHANGES
- `response.headers["Set-Cookie"]` (and any repeated header) is "v1, v2",
  not the last value.
- An https response with no Content-Length and no chunking from a server
  that closes TCP without close_notify now fails as truncated.

2026-10-07 (batch 9):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 9: measured performance fixes)

Regression test: tests/test_audit_w3_native_9.js.

- Interpreter: `local += x` with one int and one double operand is inline
  (`sum += i` once `sum` is fractional: 65 ms -> 31 ms per 3M iterations).
  Conditional branches poll for interrupts only when they jump backward.
  JetStream subset, interleaved best of 3: richards -3%, deltablue -2%,
  raytrace, crypto, navier-stokes and splay within 1%.
- `new Float64Array(array)` (any typed array from an array) no longer walks
  the iterator protocol per element when the array iterator is the built-in
  one: 120 ms -> 28 ms for 4M elements.
- DataFrame: `FROM_RECORDS` matches keys by identity instead of comparing
  names (952 ns -> 23 ns per cell at 800 columns, 104 -> 32 at 20);
  `TO_RECORDS` binds each column once (-28%).
- AESGCM / ChaCha20Poly1305 objects keep a keyed cipher context: seal/open of
  a 64-byte message 614 ns -> 311 ns; 1 MiB unchanged.

2026-10-07 (batch 8):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 8: timers, reactor, Redis, os, files, logs)

Regression test: tests/test_audit_w3_native_8.js.

- Timers: `setTimeout`/`clearTimeout` are O(log n) (a binary heap with an id
  table replaces the sorted list: 28 us -> 0.2 us per operation at 20,000 live
  timers). An interval no longer consumes the live-timer cap each time it
  fires (after 100,000 firings `setTimeout` threw "too many live timers").
- Event loop: timers and I/O completions alternate; a burst of completions no
  longer delays a due timer until all of them are delivered (measured gap
  439 ms -> 24 ms), and a due timer still cannot starve I/O. A full thread
  pool parks further work on the reactor instead of running blocking file
  work on the loop thread (48 reads through one pool thread: 924 ms inside
  the calls -> 1 ms).
- Reactor: closing the last listener no longer stops the 250 ms tick that
  idle sweeps and query deadlines run on; a cancelled write promotes the next
  queued one; a callback that closes and reopens a descriptor cannot receive
  the old occupant's event.
- Redis: the subscription state follows the server's confirmations, so a
  message after `UNSUBSCRIBE` of a channel that was never subscribed is
  delivered to the push handler instead of resolving the next command;
  subscribe verbs are refused inside `pipeline()`; RESP3 attributes are
  accepted at any position; the password is wiped after the handshake; an
  option containing U+0000 is refused.
- PostgreSQL: `maxResultBytes` (default 1 GiB) bounds one result set.
- `os.exec`: arguments, `file` and `cwd` containing U+0000 are refused;
  children start with the default SIGPIPE disposition. `os.kill` refuses
  pid 0 and -1 and out-of-range signal numbers.
- `copyFile`: a source that is not a regular file is refused; a failed copy
  removes the destination it created; `move` across filesystems syncs the
  copy before removing the source.
- Logger: files are created 0640; sinks shared between runtimes are
  serialised.
- Watcher (kqueue): descriptors of deleted entries are released; arming is a
  hash lookup.
- `dyna:bench`: samples are batches lasting at least 0.1 ms, so a
  sub-microsecond body no longer reports a median of 0.
- `dyna:async`: Semaphore/Queue/Channel/Pool hand-offs are O(1).
- `App.rpc/static/upload/ws/sse/proxy` return the app. `App.proxy` always
  sends an origin-form target upstream and closes the upstream connection
  when its client goes away.
- Spawn: reading a full pipe buffer in small pieces is linear.
- dyna:ml: a persisted tree model whose hyper-parameters are out of range, or
  whose family flags do not match the class, is refused; LinearRegression
  takes at most 4096 features.
- Raw-mode prompts restore the terminal on SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGQUIT and at exit.
- A Worker's undelivered messages are capped at 256 MiB.

BEHAVIOR CHANGES
- `os.kill(0, sig)` and `os.kill(-1, sig)` throw a RangeError.
- A child of `os.exec` that writes to a closed pipe dies of SIGPIPE, as it
  would from a shell (it used to inherit SIG_IGN and see EPIPE).
- `copyFile` from a device or FIFO throws a TypeError.
- New log files are 0640 instead of 0644.
- `redis.pipeline()` throws a TypeError for subscribe verbs.
- `bench` p50Ms/p99Ms are per-batch means for bodies faster than 0.1 ms.

2026-10-07 (batch 7):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 7, small items)

Regression test: tests/test_audit_w3_native_7.js.

- SQLite: `exec()` reports 0 for statements that change no rows (it returned
  the previous statement's count: `create table` after a 3-row insert gave
  3); SQL containing U+0000 is refused (SQLite silently stopped reading
  there); handles open in defensive mode with trusted_schema off.
- PostgreSQL: SQL containing U+0000 is refused; an int8 text value that is
  out of range or not in canonical form stays text instead of saturating; the
  command-tag row count no longer overflows.
- DNS: an encoded name is limited to 255 octets including the root label (256
  was accepted); a TTL with the top bit set is 0 (RFC 2181).
- HTTP server: a request line must end in HTTP/1.0 or HTTP/1.1 and its method
  must be a token; anything else is a 400 (`GET /x HTTP/9.9` was served).
- term Table/StyleText: at most 4096 columns / 256 styles, checked before
  any allocation.
- CSV file reads use read(2), like the other file readers.

BEHAVIOR CHANGES
- `db.exec("create table ...")` returns 0 where it could return a stale count.
- Requests with an HTTP version other than 1.0/1.1 get a 400.

2026-10-07 (batch 6):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 6)

- HTTPClient/fetch: a buffered exchange ends with a timeout error after
  20 x the socket timeout in total (10 minutes at the 30 s default). The
  timeout used to apply to each read only, so a server sending one byte at a
  time could hold a request open indefinitely. getStream() is unchanged.
  Test: tests/test_audit_w3_native_6.js.
- App.ws(): new optional `upgrade(req)` handler, called before the 101
  response with the handshake's Origin, Host, Cookie and subprotocol. A falsy
  return or a throw answers 403. WebSocket routes had no way to see the
  upgrade request, so a cookie-authenticated endpoint could not reject a
  cross-site page (cross-site WebSocket hijacking).

2026-10-07 (batch 5):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 5)

Regression test: tests/test_audit_w3_native_5.js (mock and live PostgreSQL 18).

- PostgreSQL: `tls: true` now performs the protocol's SSLRequest step and
  starts TLS only on the server's `S`. It used to open with a TLS ClientHello,
  which no stock server accepts, so TLS could not be used at all. An `N`
  answer, or any byte after `S`, ends the connection; there is no fallback to
  plaintext.
- dyna:simd: a window `offset`/`length`/`limit` beyond 2^53 is a RangeError
  (2^64+1 wrapped to 0 and returned a sum). Fractions still truncate.
- Workers: at most 256 live Workers; creating another is a RangeError.
- Docs: Brotli `level` is ignored by the macOS system codec.

2026-10-07 (batch 4):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 4)

Regression tests: tests/test_audit_w3_native_4.js, tests/test_install_flow.sh.

- dyna:ml: `fit()` obeys `--timeout-ms` (LogisticRegression, KMeans,
  GaussianMixture, SVC and every tree/forest/boosting estimator). A long fit
  used to run until the hard stop killed the process (exit 113); it now ends
  with the "interrupted" error.
- Redis: `HELLO` and `CLIENT REPLY` are refused, alone or in a pipeline. They
  change how many replies arrive, which silently attached later replies to
  the wrong commands. Verified against the mock and a live Redis.
- install.sh: the freshly built binary must run and pass the SHA-256 known
  answer BEFORE it replaces the installed one; a wrong digest is fatal (it
  was reported as a successful install); the binary is installed by rename,
  so a failed run leaves the previous one intact.
- Repository: `.obj-t262/` (a build directory already listed in .gitignore
  but committed earlier) is no longer tracked.

BEHAVIOR CHANGES
- `redis.command("HELLO", ...)` and `redis.command("CLIENT", "REPLY", ...)` throw.

2026-10-07 (later):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 3): numeric correctness

Regression tests: tests/test_audit_w3_simd.js, tests/test_audit_w3_mathx.js.
Expected values are definitions evaluated in plain JS, DLMF identities and
Abramowitz & Stegun table values.

dyna:simd
- sum, mean, dot, normL1, variance (and normalize's statistics) accumulate in
  float64. They were summed in float32 lanes: `sum` of 2e7 copies of 0.1 was
  4.3% low, 8e7 ones summed to 67108864. They are also about 2x faster on
  large arrays (0.28 -> 0.14 ns per element for sum).
- min/max of an all-infinite array returns that infinity (was +-FLT_MAX).
- distCheb returns NaN for a NaN anywhere (short inputs dropped it).
- topkIndices no longer stalls on a NaN; NaN ranks lowest.
- argmax/argmin ignore NaN on every instruction set; cummax carries the
  running maximum across a NaN.
- silu gives one answer per input value whatever the array length (the last
  n mod lanes elements used a different exponential).
- x86: silu(x <= -89.42) returned x and vexp(x >= 89.42) returned 0.
- arm64: vinv of a positive subnormal returned -Infinity; vsqrt, vinv and
  vrsqrt are now correctly rounded (they were estimate + Newton steps).
- gemv/gemvT/gemm refuse an output that overlaps an input (the result was
  silently wrong).

dyna:mathx
- gammainc, gammaincinv, betainc: the series budget grows with the shape and
  a series that still does not converge returns NaN. `gammainc(1e5, 1e5)` was
  0.398 (true 0.5004); `betainc(0.5, 1e8, 1e8)` was 0.385 (true 0.5).
- besseli/besseliScaled: correct for x > 20 with a moderate or large order.
  `besseli(30, 21)` was -2.3e9 (true 0.4798).
- scalbn/ldexp: exponents beyond int32 saturate instead of wrapping.
- erfcinv: accurate down to 1e-300 (was Infinity below about 1e-16).
- besselk of an order above 1e6 at x < 0.5 is +Infinity (was 0); bessely
  refuses orders above 2^24 like besselj; legendreP no longer converts an
  out-of-range order to int before rejecting it.

BEHAVIOR CHANGES
- float32 reductions return slightly different (more accurate) values for
  long arrays.
- argmax/argmin/topkIndices/cummax results change for inputs containing NaN.
- besselk/besselkScaled of an order above 1e6 (or non-finite) return
  +Infinity, the mathematical limit, where they returned 0. The guard against
  an O(order) recurrence is unchanged.
- gemv/gemvT/gemm with an aliased output now throw.
- vsqrt/vinv/vrsqrt low bits change on arm64.

2026-10-07:

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 2)

Regression test: tests/test_audit_w3_native_3.js.

- SQLite: a running statement is interrupted when `--timeout-ms` expires (it
  used to run on until the hard stop killed the process), and the native
  memory limit is SQLite's heap limit (400 MB was storable under a 16 MB
  budget).
- `os.exec` with `timeout`: the child runs in its own process group, the
  whole group is signalled and the child is reaped. A timed-out command used
  to leave a zombie and its own children running.
- `readFile`/`readBytes` (sync and async) read with read(2). They used to map
  files of 64 KiB and up, so another process truncating the file killed the
  reader with SIGBUS.
- dyna:log text format escapes C1 controls (U+0080..U+009F), DEL and
  U+2028/U+2029, as the JSON format already did.
- `makeTempFile`/`makeTempDir`: a prefix with `/`, `\`, a leading `.` or over
  200 bytes is a RangeError (`"../../x"` created files outside the temp dir).
- `sys.setUid`/`setGid`: a failing `setgroups` is an error instead of being
  ignored.
- Scrypt: `p` is part of both budgets (memory 128*r*(N+p+2) <= 1 GiB, work
  N*r*p <= 2^26).
- Argon2id is about 2.4x faster (65536 KiB, t=3: 264 ms -> 112 ms): the
  per-block scratch wipe is one memset instead of 2048 volatile byte stores.
  Output is unchanged.
- Template: the 65536-tag compile cap is reported as such (was "out of
  memory"); a section's output pre-reservation is clamped to 16 MiB.
- Selector: the match budget is 2^24 unit tests (was 2^28) and the memoised
  path is charged too.

BEHAVIOR CHANGES
- Temp-name prefixes containing a separator or starting with `.` now throw.
- Scrypt with N*r*p above 2^26 now throws.
- A selector that needed more than 2^24 unit tests now throws the budget error.

2026-10-06 (later):

## Audit hardening, wave 3 (batch 1)

Regression tests: tests/test_audit_w3_native_1.js, tests/test_audit_w3_native_2.js.

Sandbox budgets
- `--native-memory-limit` / `sys.setNativeMemoryLimit` now count every
  native-module allocation, not six files' worth: a 5 MB gzip bomb under a
  1 MB native budget peaked at 861 MB of RSS and now stops at 13 MB. Counting
  starts when a limit is first set; with no limit the cost is one flag test
  per allocation. What is and is not counted is stated at setNativeMemoryLimit.
- A timer that is always due (a `setTimeout(f, 0)` chain, an interval slower
  than its body) no longer starves sockets, file completions and Workers:
  I/O is polled at least every 2 ms of back-to-back timers.

Process lifetime
- `Spawn.wait()` and `Argon2id.hashAsync/verifyAsync` now take part in the
  engine-shutdown sweep like every other parked operation: if the runtime is
  torn down while one is pending (for example after an uncaught error) its
  promise is rejected with "<op>: aborted at engine shutdown" and the process
  exits with its normal status. It used to abort (SIGABRT, status 134) on an
  engine assertion.

Compression and archives
- `maxOutputBytes` is exact for every decoder (was refused below 320 bytes of
  slack for gunzip/inflate, 8 for lz4, 65536 for unzstd). A frame that
  declares more than the cap is a RangeError like every other cap refusal
  (unzstd and unsnappy threw TypeError).
- `unzstd` decodes concatenated frames; `unzstd`, `unbrotli` and `lz4Unframe`
  refuse bytes after the end of the stream instead of ignoring them.
- `ZipExtractAll` writes nothing unless the whole archive validates and every
  member passes its CRC; members that share archive bytes are refused; a
  member cannot inflate past its declared size.
- `ZipList` refuses a central directory longer than the declared count.
- tar: `.` and `a/.` are accepted names; an over-long GNU long name is refused
  instead of truncated.

Network reactor
- A listener whose accept fails for lack of descriptors or memory is paused
  and retried from the 250 ms sweep instead of spinning a core (Linux; Darwin
  drops the connection itself). At most 256 accepts per dispatch.
- A DNS completion for a socket that was closed and whose descriptor number
  was reused is discarded (it could connect the new socket to the old host).
- Accepted sockets and Worker wake pipes are close-on-exec.
- `sendfile` on a file that became shorter than the promised length ends with
  an error instead of spinning (Darwin) or reporting success (Linux).
- A send issued while a file body is still being sent is queued behind it.

BEHAVIOR CHANGES
- Trailing bytes after a zstd/brotli/lz4-frame stream now throw.
- `unzstd(a ++ b)` returns both frames' contents.
- zstd/snappy declared-size refusals are RangeError (were TypeError).
- `ZipList` on an archive whose end record under-declares its entries throws.
- Exit status for "uncaught error with a pending Spawn.wait or async Argon2"
  is 1 (was 134).

2026-10-06:

## Audit hardening, wave 2

Each item has a regression test (tests/test_audit_w2_*.js); bounds and refusal
rules are stated in dynajs.d.ts.

Sandbox budgets
- `--timeout-ms` now holds for tail-call loops, for loops whose back-edge is a
  fused compare-and-branch (`do {} while (x === 1)`), for `tryCatch` (which no
  longer catches the interrupt), for `Object.equals`/`clone`/`mergeDeep`/`evolve`,
  for the array-like forms of `with`/`toReversed`/`toSpliced`/`toSorted`, and for
  an event loop that is only waiting. A script past its deadline exits non-zero.
- Hard stop: if the script has not stopped within the deadline plus a grace of
  timeout/4 (clamped to 250..2000 ms) the process is terminated with exit code
  113. This is the bound for native loops that do not poll.
- An `await` that can never settle (no timers, I/O or jobs left) is an
  InternalError instead of a busy spin.
- A Worker's native stack is 8 MiB on every platform; runaway recursion inside
  a Worker is a RangeError, not a crash.

Memory safety
- A buffer handed to a native call cannot be detached, resized or transferred
  until that call returns (see BEHAVIOR CHANGES).
- Native modules build result objects and arrays with own properties only, so
  an accessor on Object.prototype or Array.prototype can no longer run user
  code in the middle of a native operation (URLSearchParams iteration, ML
  fitted attributes and fold tables, and every other result builder).
- structures: `X.deserialize()` constructs natively and never consults
  `X.prototype.constructor` (heap overflow in every decoder).
- matcher: `Levenshtein(a, b)` without `{max}` no longer overflows or returns a
  wrong distance when the shorter string exceeds 64 code points.
- decimal: an allocation failure under `--native-memory-limit` is an exception,
  not a NULL dereference.
- dataframe: a SharedArrayBuffer-backed column is snapshotted at construction;
  a shared mask is refused. ml: shared Float64Array input is refused.
- ml: fold indices are range-checked; a Pipeline cannot be closed by one of its
  own stages mid-fit.
- crypto: PEM text outlives the BIO that parses it; growable SharedArrayBuffer
  allocation; TLS client connections keep their context alive.
- file: `removeAll` no longer closes its directory descriptor twice.
- `Object.assocPath`/`dissocPath`/`modifyPath` on a runaway path throw instead of
  overflowing the native stack.

Denial of service
- Atom table and Map: a flooded hash chain switches that table to a keyed hash
  (crafted equal-length keys no longer make interning quadratic).
- String search (`indexOf`, `lastIndexOf`, `includes`, `split`, `count`, and
  the byte search behind bytes/matcher/regexp prefilter/http) falls back to a
  linear-time algorithm when a naive scan stops making progress.
- URL: `pathname` setter is linear; an IDNA host over 4096 mapped code points
  is refused. markdown: delimiter runs, backtick runs, link tails and
  blockquotes are all inside the work budget. dataframe: one NaN no longer makes
  ROLLING_VAR/ROLLING_STD quadratic. `Object.keysIn`/`valuesIn`/`omit` are
  O(n log n).

Hardening
- crypto: keys and secrets must be a string or bytes (TypeError otherwise);
  JWTVerify refuses a PEM key for HS*, an empty HMAC key, a `crit` header and a
  non-canonical signature; ES256/384/512 require the exact curve; an encrypted
  PEM is refused without prompting; X509.generateSelfSigned uses a random
  serial and refuses SAN entries containing a comma or line break.
- http: static routes refuse dot-segments per path segment, hide dotfiles
  (except /.well-known/) and open files beneath the root without following
  symlinks; client chunk sizes are strict hex; a header string needs a token name.
- net: TCPProxy defaults to 8192 connections and a 10 s upstream connect
  timeout, and takes a `host` option.
- sys: spawned children close every inherited descriptor (close_range/closefrom
  where available), drop supplementary groups before setgid/setuid, and the
  uid/gid value 4294967295 is refused.
- html: attributes past the 512 cap are discarded, not re-parsed as markup;
  selector attribute tests read own attributes only; over-deep hand-built trees
  are a RangeError.

## BEHAVIOR CHANGES (wave 2)

- ArrayBuffer `resize`/`transfer`/`transferToFixedLength` and
  `Worker.postMessage` transfer throw TypeError ("in use by a running native
  call") when invoked from a getter or callback while a native function still
  holds that buffer. Do the transfer after the call returns.
- `String.prototype.escapeHTML` also escapes `"` and `'` (as `&quot;`, `&#39;`).
- `Object.set(obj, path, v)` creates and writes OWN properties only; a path
  through `__proto__` or `constructor.prototype` creates plain own keys.
- `String.prototype.format` reads own properties of its object argument only.
- crypto key/secret arguments: `undefined`, `null`, numbers, booleans and plain
  objects are a TypeError (they were stringified).
- `X509.generateSelfSigned` serial numbers are random (was always 01).
- `ml.KMeans(k, { seed })` now honours the seed (the object was read as seed 0).
- dataframe `TO_CSV` prints numbers in shortest round-trip form (`0.1`, not
  `0.10000000000000001`; `Infinity`, not `inf`) and puts the formula guard
  inside the quotes.
- `--timeout-ms`: exit code is non-zero once the deadline has passed, 113 on
  the hard stop.
- http static routes: dotfiles answer 404.

2026-10-03:

## Audit hardening

Security fixes from the full-tree audit (each with its regression test; the
exact bounds and refusal rules live in dynajs.d.ts and API.md):

- PostgreSQL MD5 auth: a crafted server challenge overflowed the heap. MD5
  and cleartext auth are now refused on a plaintext connection unless the
  per-connection `insecureAuth: true` opt-in is set (`tls: true` accepts
  them without the knob).
- URL redaction overflow: Fetcher stats() echoed the stored proxy URL and CA
  verbatim. Proxy userinfo is redacted to `***@` and a PEM `ca` is returned
  as `<pem-redacted>`.
- onConnect use-after-free: HTTPClient's connect hook could outlive a client
  closed mid-request; the hook state is now ownership-tracked.
- SpawnPipe detach use-after-free: detaching a Spawn stdin/stdout pipe
  mid-operation no longer touches freed pipe state.
- Decode-bomb budgets: structures deserialize (plus ASN.1 decode and the
  record decoders) charges at most 512 MiB + 64x the record's payload bytes
  of declared native allocation per record and refuses the rest instead of
  allocating.
- Verify caps: Argon2id.verify refuses PHC parameters over 16 iterations,
  16 parallelism or 1 GiB memory (8*parallelism <= m KiB <= 1048576);
  PBKDF2 caps iterations x output-blocks at 2^24.
- DoS caps: regexp/selector compile and markdown render budgets, template
  render 1048576 elements, IDNA labels 4096 mapped code points, punycode
  decode 1024 octets, URL input 64 KiB, TOTP window 4096, bcrypt password
  72 bytes, Metrics registry 256 series, Sitemap.list 1000 children /
  50000 URLs.
- CRLF request injection: Fetcher header names must be HTTP tokens and
  values must not contain CR/LF/NUL, on every path (get/getAsync/robots/
  Crawl/getStream).

## BREAKING / BEHAVIOR CHANGES (0.9 -> 0.10)

Before/after guidance for each item: API.md, section "0.9 -> 0.10 migration".

- crypto.PBKDF2: `salt` is REQUIRED; a missing salt is a TypeError (the old
  all-zero default was silently weak).
- http.CookieSerialize: `opts` is typed `CookieOptions` (was
  `Record<string, unknown>`); the bag is strict, so an unknown key throws.
- http.RangeParse: declared `[{start,end},...] | "unsatisfiable" | null`
  (was `unknown`); a header may carry several ranges, and "unsatisfiable"
  is a distinct outcome from a malformed (null) one.
- ml: SVC.decisionFunction, predictProba, PCA.fitTransform and the scalers'
  transform/fitTransform may return a flat Float64Array when X is flat.
- ml.PipelineStage: DBScan removed (it fits but cannot transform/predict, so
  a Pipeline has no position for it).
- xml.XMLStringify refuses raw NUL (XML 1.0 Char excludes U+0000).
- config.TOML.stringify refuses null/undefined (TOML 1.0 has no null
  literal).
- time.Duration: every component is bounded at |value| <= 1e12; a larger
  component is a RangeError (keeps the downstream int64 folds in range).
- structures: serialized-record decode budget is 512 MiB + 64x the payload
  per record; amplified encodings are additionally capped at their legitimate
  scale (constants/UnionFind 2^23, arithmetic sorted/BitSet/Bloom/CMS words
  2^22), so over-amplified records are refused, not allocated.
- http.App.upload: a declared Content-Type outside a non-empty `allow` list
  is now 415 (was 403); `.ext` entries never match an upload.
- config.Env.load: a NUL value is a TypeError and an expansion above 1 MiB a
  RangeError; the file is capped at 8 MiB and keys at 4096 bytes.
- compress: every decompressor gained the optional `maxOutputBytes` cap
  (integer 1..2^30; RangeError when exceeded, omitted = the 1 GiB default).
- scrape.Sitemap.list: an index over 1000 child sitemaps and a flattened
  result over 50000 URLs are RangeErrors.
- matcher: the diff budget is widened to 2*(len_a+len_b)^2 diagonal steps
  clamped to [2^28, 2^31], so wide dissimilar inputs now diff instead of
  refusing early.
- structures.IntervalTree: insert(lo, hi) refuses an inverted pair with
  RangeError (0.9 clamped hi up to lo), and deserialize skips a forged
  inverted interval the same way it skips a NaN row.

2026-09-04:

- dyn-ac (audit E10-11 redesign): MEASURED (bench/bench_ac.c, old build vs
  new linked against the same harness, hits and text hashes asserted equal on
  every row). The single dense table (1 KiB/state, 99.6% redundant slots --
  fill = edges/(states*256) = 0.004 on every bench set) is now one of two
  materializations of the same trie, chosen at build time by
  DYN_AC_DENSE_MAX (2048 states, the measured cache crossover):
    small sets stay dense  -- S10/A2/P1/L64 scan 0.97-1.05x = parity
    large sets go compact (double-array, ~31 B/state, fail-walk scan)
      M1000   6882 states:  6.8 MiB -> 180 KiB (38x), build 3.5x faster,
                            scan 1.62x / 2.65x / 1.43x FASTER (many/none/near)
      X10000 75364 states: 74.5 MiB -> 2.3 MiB (33x), build 4.4x faster,
                            scan 3.16x / 2.63x / 2.95x FASTER
    cap boundary (262144 states, the pinned 2^18 formula): 261 MiB -> 10.0 MiB
    steady (26x), cap-scan 2.98 -> 1.12 ns/B; build 57.7 -> 55.8 ms.
  Why the scan got FASTER with the memory cut: the dense table at 75k states
  is 75 MiB and the scan is cache/TLB-bound on it (25-34 MB/s); the compact
  table is ~2 MiB and the same text runs at ~100 MB/s. The root row stays
  fully materialized in both layouts, which is what bounds the compact fail
  walk (each step strictly decreases depth; total fail steps <= text length
  on ANY input, adversarial included). The 2-symbol, single-char and 60-byte
  shared-prefix adversarial sets are rows in the bench; the -1/OOM cap
  contract and the exact state-count formula in test_ac_caps.c are untouched
  and green. Differential: bench/bench_ac.c --diff (engine vs the pinned
  reference implementation in bench/ac_cand.c) is 42/42 over the systematic
  corpus, also green forced all-dense and all-compact, under ASan+UBSan;
  bench_core --hash is identical old vs new (2012816943).

2026-08-28:

- dyna:log: MEASURED (tests/bench_log_ab.js, old build vs new, controls flat
  at 26 ns empty-loop / 46 ns silent-gate). The honest table:
    short msg     643 -> 633 ns   (-1.5%)
    small fields  882 -> 894 ns   (+1.4%)
    40 fields    3168 -> 3404 ns  (+7.4%)   <-- a real regression
    err+fields    799 -> 1154 ns  (+44%)    <-- the Error-extras feature
    inline 100b   689 -> 675 ns   (-2%)
  The inline line buffer is a WASH, not the win the design assumed, and the
  reason is measured: an emit is SYSCALL-BOUND. stderr costs 630 ns/op while
  the same serialization into a buffered file costs 95 ns/op and the level
  gate alone is 30 ns, so ~600 ns of every emit is write(2). THE LEVER IS
  `buffer: true` (or a file dest), which is 6.6x. The 40-field regression is
  NOT the reserved-key check: disabling it entirely (3404 ns) and removing
  the branch (3381 ns) both leave ~7%, so it is code-layout/inlining in the
  larger translation unit, left as a known cost rather than chased.
- dyna:log: a truncated line is now CLOSED instead of severed. The cap cut
  the line at exactly 64 KiB, which for a long payload stopped mid-string
  with no closing quote, no closing brace and no newline -- emitting
  malformed JSON that also corrupted the NEXT line's framing, since a
  consumer could not find this one's end. The tail now reserves room and the
  emit path writes `...` in-band before closing, so an oversized payload
  yields one parseable line (property-tested). Fixed two JS_ToCString leaks
  in the rollover option parsers (one per Logger built with a string
  size/frequency). Verified clean under ASan+UBSan+LSan and warning-free.
- dyna:log redesign around the pino/sonic-boom, zap/zerolog and logrotate
  playbooks. Emit path is allocation-free for typical lines (512-byte inline
  line builder, heap only past it); timestamps render without snprintf (i64
  writer for epoch, cached-second ISO with manual millis); int64 round-trip
  fast path for number fields. New file destination (dest/mkdir/buffer) with
  sonic-boom-style line batching, a process-wide sink registry so Loggers on
  one path share fd + rollover state (config mismatch refused), flush(), and
  an atexit+finalizer flush belt. Rollover (size in bytes or k/m/g, frequency
  daily/hourly/ms, count retention, symlink to the active file) is lazy per
  emit, extension-last names (app.2026-08-28.1.log) that sort
  chronologically, numbering continues from the highest existing file so
  retention never causes number reuse, time-only rollover reuses the period's
  file across restarts, failed rotation falls back to stderr with a 1/s retry.
  Format integrity: caller fields colliding with frame keys (time, level,
  name, pid, hostname, msg, err) are dropped -- log forgery by field name is
  impossible; (err, fields, msg) shape added; Error extras (code, errno...)
  ride inside err; pid/hostname enrichment folded into the pre-serialized
  prefix. New human format: format:"text" renders aligned levels, name,
  message and k=v fields in one control-escaped line. Debug: node-debug
  matching (last match wins, '-' negation, '*' anywhere), multi-arg join.

2026-08-27:

- dyna:scrape modernization pass. Robots.txt (RFC 9309): group-based parser
  with product-token agent matching -- exact full-agent match beats the
  longest token match, which beats `*`; consecutive User-agent lines share
  one group; groups capped at 64 with up to 8 UA lines. Fetcher: RFC 3986
  redirect resolution (relative, dot-segment, protocol-relative, cross-host,
  each hop re-gated by robots and the SSRF check); fragment-only Location
  ends the chain instead of erroring; conditional GET against stored ETag /
  Last-Modified validators (32 entries, 1 MiB/body, 4 MiB total, FIFO) --
  a 304 returns status 304 with the cached body, fromCache and notModified
  set, counted in stats.revalidated and stats.savedBytes; caller `headers`
  merged under the crawler's identity, credential keys (Authorization,
  Cookie, Proxy-Authorization, Set-Cookie) refused at construction because
  they would leak to redirect targets; jittered exponential backoff whose
  server-provided Retry-After is never jittered. Crawl: relative linkField
  hrefs resolve against the emitting page; non-navigational schemes dropped;
  pending frontier capped at 10000. Extractor: trim, per-field default
  (satisfies optional required fields), source: true reading raw child
  source for script/style/ld+json bodies HTMLText refuses to serve as text,
  as: "url" resolving against run()'s base, as: "json" refusing invalid
  input, and ANY unknown as: string refusing at construction.
- Final honesty round: Retry-After delays BEYOND the 60s sleep cap are now
  served as the final response instead of sleeping 60s and retrying anyway
  (violating the server's instruction); the fuzz section's Fetcher forgot
  minDelayMs:0 and ran at 1 exec/s under the default 1000ms per-host floor
  (2000 runs = 33 min -- fixed, now ~90 exec/s and verified clean at 6000+
  runs; RSS growth proven to be the pre-existing per-context harness
  baseline, same or worse on fuzz_stdlib which has no scrape section);
  cache content-type strdup failure now guarded like the validators.
  New coverage: over-long Location served as final, robots redirect loop
  fail-closed, maxPages:0, Retry-After beyond cap honored-by-refusal.
- Seven-bug hardening round on dyna:scrape: (1) Retry-After HTTP-date
  deltas compared epoch-to-monotonic -- every date-form retry slept a full
  60s instead of the seconds asked; now compared against time(NULL), with a
  dt<15s regression bound so a clock mix fails LOUD. (2) ex_node_source
  leaked its chunks on the OOM path. (3) request-url fragments stripped
  before robots, the wire, and dedup keys. (4) Crawl link-following gated to
  2xx while VALUE extraction still runs on 403/500 pages -- bad pages stay
  scrapable without being trusted to point anywhere. (5) robots.txt UTF-8
  BOM no longer orphans the first group. (6) robots redirect comment/code
  aligned (2 redirects, 3 requests). (7) verified non-bugs: Cache-Control
  scan has no multi-char constants; canonical OOM-ignore is benign.
- Deeper irregularity round on dyna:scrape: Retry-After honoured as an
  IMF-fixdate HTTP-date, not just delay-seconds (the old strtod read dates
  as 0 and silently ignored them); Cache-Control: no-store excluded from
  the conditional-GET store; robots.txt redirects followed (<=3 hops, RFC
  9309 2.3.1.2) with fail-closed treatment of unsafe hops -- canonical-host
  301s no longer disallow whole sites; Crawl canonicalField (duplicate
  canonical pages emit but queue no links; no phantom-seen marking) and
  relField (nofollow skipping); Extractor default now also replaces an
  EMPTY all-array. Refcount fixes found by exit-time leak dump: value
  object leaked when a page emitted AT the depth cap (free sat inside the
  depth gate), and the pre-existing as:"number" dup-never-freed string leak
  -- both invisible to gates running ASan with detect_leaks=0; a boundary
  regression case now covers the shape. SSRF doc honesty: dyn:http's
  connect does no address-class check today, so DNS rebinding passes the
  name-shape gate; docs now say so and point at egress allowlisting.
- Page/protocol irregularity pass on dyna:scrape: https->http redirect
  downgrades refused by default (allowInsecureDowngrade opts in); declared
  Content-Length refused against maxBodyBytes BEFORE transfer (received-bytes
  cap still enforced, so lying headers stay bounded); every response carries
  the origin's Content-Type verbatim and a revalidation hit replays the
  STORED type; Crawl baseField honors a page's <base href> declaration
  (relative declarations resolve against the page; cross-host bases need
  sameHost:false); documented source-only posture -- no JS evaluation, so
  meta-refresh and SPA rendering are invisible by design.
- New hostile-page coverage: tag-soup recovery through extractor fields,
  duplicate-id multi-match, entity-encoded hrefs through as:"url", nesting
  bombs refused at HTMLParse's bound, downgrade/CL refusals, cached-type
  replay. fuzz_parsers drives Robots bodies + agents, a 30x Location and
  Extractor option strings from fuzz input; its smoke runs now seed from
  corpus_robots.
- Pre-existing gate blockers fixed en route: dyna-oauth2.c compiled with
  eight unused-variable warnings (native sanitizer builds are zero-warning
  gated) and API.md's Crawl example dereferenced p.value without an
  extractor (check-api executes all 291 blocks).

2026-08-21:

- HTTP hardening pass (dyna:net client, App, HTTPServer, HTTPServerAsync):
  header/method CRLF injection refused; hostile status lines clamp instead
  of overflowing; WS opening handshake enforces RFC 6455 4.2.1; route
  status/contentType validated at registration; prefix routes end at a
  path segment; static files served from an O_NOFOLLOW fd with HEAD and
  single-range (206/416) support; upload holds its handler by value;
  async RPC settles through a promise capability; JSON-RPC error bodies
  are escaped and bounded; notifications (no id) get no response.
- Connection lifetime: App honors Connection: close and the HTTP/1.0
  default-close, closing on the response's own send completion; requests
  pipelined behind a close go unanswered. Client rejects responses with
  both Transfer-Encoding and Content-Length.
- Resource bounds: App maxConns (default 8192); over-cap declared bodies
  answer 413; HTTPServerAsync answers 431 at the header cap; ws/sse/ws-
  client outbound queues bounded at 4 MB via dyn_aio_queued(); thread-pool
  server gains an absolute requestTimeoutMs deadline (default 30s);
  accept-error storms pause instead of spinning; io_uring SQE exhaustion
  harvests completions instead of stalling connections.
- IO: one writev per thread-pool response (+7-8% req/s vs prior tree at
  c=64/256); client threads the chunked framing flag instead of rescanning
  headers; connect poll timeout clamped; IPv6 literals bind dual-stack
  (App grows a host option).
